WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:18.547 --> 00:00:20.554
- [Narrator] Mankind and the elements.

00:00:20.554 --> 00:00:23.355
For some, it's an uncomfortable bond.

00:00:23.355 --> 00:00:24.879
- It's not really a
good idea to have divers

00:00:24.879 --> 00:00:26.921
in the water with a thunderstorm.

00:00:26.921 --> 00:00:29.223
- [Narrator] For others,
when weather strikes,

00:00:29.223 --> 00:00:31.634
inspiration begins.

00:00:31.634 --> 00:00:35.725
- If they have a catastrophe,
we can be there to help them.

00:00:35.725 --> 00:00:38.679
- [Narrator] These are the
people who challenge nature,

00:00:38.679 --> 00:00:42.508
seek out its limits, reveal its secrets,

00:00:42.508 --> 00:00:45.008
and embrace its awesome power.

00:00:46.715 --> 00:00:49.572
In this episode, we'll meet an elite team

00:00:49.572 --> 00:00:52.475
of diver-scientists using
an underwater laboratory

00:00:52.475 --> 00:00:55.159
as a home base for scientific research.

00:00:55.159 --> 00:00:58.578
- Our scientists can spend
days and weeks underwater.

00:00:58.578 --> 00:01:00.301
- [Narrator] A man
growing millions of trees

00:01:00.301 --> 00:01:02.734
to be planted across
the devastated forests

00:01:02.734 --> 00:01:04.870
of the American west.

00:01:04.870 --> 00:01:06.592
- I'll always work with plants.

00:01:06.592 --> 00:01:08.322
There's no if, ands, or buts.

00:01:08.322 --> 00:01:10.724
- [Narrator] And in Boulder,
Colorado, an extraordinarily

00:01:10.724 --> 00:01:12.571
gifted rock balancer.

00:01:12.571 --> 00:01:14.758
- My approach was to experiment.

00:01:14.758 --> 00:01:17.313
What happens if I add one more rock?

00:01:17.313 --> 00:01:19.652
- [Narrator] These pioneers
of the great outdoors ahead

00:01:19.652 --> 00:01:21.152
on That's Amazing.

00:01:30.218 --> 00:01:32.885
(calming music)

00:01:35.520 --> 00:01:36.947
- [Narrator] If you find
yourself in the creeks

00:01:36.947 --> 00:01:41.385
of Boulder, Colorado, you
may come across this man.

00:01:41.385 --> 00:01:43.808
- Yoda is like one of the grand masters,

00:01:43.808 --> 00:01:46.195
and one on of his major things was,

00:01:46.195 --> 00:01:48.976
"Try not, only do, there is no try."

00:01:48.976 --> 00:01:49.831
- [Narrator] Michael Grab is a

00:01:49.831 --> 00:01:51.581
rock balancing extraordinaire,

00:01:51.581 --> 00:01:52.414
and a prominent member of
a growing global community.

00:01:52.414 --> 00:01:55.769
- [Michael] That's kind of a philosophical

00:01:55.769 --> 00:01:58.633
approach to all of this is you just do it

00:01:58.633 --> 00:02:00.217
and you don't think about it.

00:02:00.217 --> 00:02:01.090
- [Narrator] He's determined to master

00:02:01.090 --> 00:02:02.322
this natural art form,

00:02:02.322 --> 00:02:05.303
and his approach and
discipline boggles the mind.

00:02:05.303 --> 00:02:06.929
- If you think too much about it,

00:02:06.929 --> 00:02:10.262
then it collapses and you break fingers.

00:02:16.553 --> 00:02:17.717
My name is Michael Grab,

00:02:17.717 --> 00:02:19.592
and I'm a stone balance artist.

00:02:19.592 --> 00:02:22.699
I just balance rocks everyday.

00:02:22.699 --> 00:02:24.468
I just started doing it randomly one day

00:02:24.468 --> 00:02:27.987
in Boulder Creek about eight years ago,

00:02:27.987 --> 00:02:29.997
and it just kind of turned

00:02:29.997 --> 00:02:33.090
into a therapeutic kind of
activity, practice, ritual,

00:02:33.090 --> 00:02:35.090
so I just kept doing it.

00:02:37.428 --> 00:02:38.788
Boulder is a really beautiful place

00:02:38.788 --> 00:02:40.324
to wake up in the morning.

00:02:40.324 --> 00:02:42.484
There's just a special
feeling about being here,

00:02:42.484 --> 00:02:43.757
especially when I'm going
to the creek every day

00:02:43.757 --> 00:02:46.812
because it's just kind
of this constant renewal

00:02:46.812 --> 00:02:49.451
of energy coming through the city.

00:02:49.451 --> 00:02:53.042
It's kind of an ideal
place for rock balancing.

00:02:53.042 --> 00:02:54.981
The whole learning process
with balancing rocks

00:02:54.981 --> 00:02:57.757
is very organic in my experience.

00:02:57.757 --> 00:03:00.059
Like I just started making tall towers,

00:03:00.059 --> 00:03:02.596
biggest rock to smallest rock,

00:03:02.596 --> 00:03:06.294
and you do that enough every day for years

00:03:06.294 --> 00:03:09.890
and eventually you start noticing nuances

00:03:09.890 --> 00:03:12.556
and you start taking it a bit
further and a bit further.

00:03:12.556 --> 00:03:15.313
It's just kind of like a
universal human activity,

00:03:15.313 --> 00:03:17.967
and that's something I find to be really

00:03:17.967 --> 00:03:19.480
fascinating about all of it.

00:03:19.480 --> 00:03:21.108
Like I thought I was the only person

00:03:21.108 --> 00:03:22.717
doing it for a long time.

00:03:22.717 --> 00:03:24.828
Then I came to learn that all these people

00:03:24.828 --> 00:03:28.003
in pretty much every
continent except Antarctica

00:03:28.003 --> 00:03:29.170
balance rocks.

00:03:30.645 --> 00:03:32.828
One of my big inspirations was

00:03:32.828 --> 00:03:35.360
a guy I saw online named Bill Dan.

00:03:35.360 --> 00:03:38.755
He's kind of the pioneer of
the whole counterbalance style.

00:03:38.755 --> 00:03:41.579
A counterbalance is just
where one rock holds

00:03:41.579 --> 00:03:43.534
another rock in place,

00:03:43.534 --> 00:03:45.142
so if that rock is removed,

00:03:45.142 --> 00:03:46.608
then the other rock falls.

00:03:46.608 --> 00:03:50.618
I usually start by looking
for a nice top rock,

00:03:50.618 --> 00:03:53.128
like a big rock I'm
going to display on top.

00:03:53.128 --> 00:03:55.610
I kind of look for the
rarest rock possible.

00:03:55.610 --> 00:03:57.623
Something with an interesting feature,

00:03:57.623 --> 00:03:59.077
like color or shape.

00:03:59.077 --> 00:04:01.402
Sometimes I'll just go where
the interesting rocks are,

00:04:01.402 --> 00:04:04.230
then I might spend an hour or two

00:04:04.230 --> 00:04:05.928
just figuring out where in that spot

00:04:05.928 --> 00:04:07.202
I want to build.

00:04:07.202 --> 00:04:11.602
That kind of drives a lot
of the creative process.

00:04:11.602 --> 00:04:14.183
Most people when they
watch me, they just see me

00:04:14.183 --> 00:04:15.992
completely frozen,

00:04:15.992 --> 00:04:19.953
but what I'm doing is, every split second,

00:04:19.953 --> 00:04:23.869
I'm juggling anywhere from
three to nine different

00:04:23.869 --> 00:04:26.657
contact points or vertices of balance,

00:04:26.657 --> 00:04:29.932
just trying to isolate
certain connections.

00:04:29.932 --> 00:04:32.878
It's like a three dimensional
gravity Rubik's cube.

00:04:32.878 --> 00:04:35.378
That's the way I visualize it.

00:04:36.234 --> 00:04:39.763
The zero point is a term I began calling

00:04:39.763 --> 00:04:43.278
the moment when I know that it's balanced

00:04:43.278 --> 00:04:44.418
and I can let go.

00:04:44.418 --> 00:04:46.370
It's kind of this infinite feeling

00:04:46.370 --> 00:04:48.322
where there's no separation between me

00:04:48.322 --> 00:04:49.822
and these objects.

00:04:55.094 --> 00:04:56.963
There's a few elements that go into like

00:04:56.963 --> 00:05:00.269
the quintessential stone balancer.

00:05:00.269 --> 00:05:03.943
There's rock selection,
location selection,

00:05:03.943 --> 00:05:05.860
and photographic skill.

00:05:06.780 --> 00:05:09.471
A lot of balancers are
strong in one aspect

00:05:09.471 --> 00:05:12.261
and weak in another, but my goal is to

00:05:12.261 --> 00:05:14.594
be exceptional in all three.

00:05:15.536 --> 00:05:16.741
I'm kind of a perfectionist.

00:05:16.741 --> 00:05:18.986
Even though the art form itself says no,

00:05:18.986 --> 00:05:21.605
you can't be a perfectionist.

00:05:21.605 --> 00:05:24.843
One of the special things
about rock balancing is

00:05:24.843 --> 00:05:26.683
you surprise yourself often

00:05:26.683 --> 00:05:28.927
if you're willing to just experiment.

00:05:28.927 --> 00:05:31.412
What happens if I add one more rock?

00:05:31.412 --> 00:05:34.370
And that's kind of the universal dilemma

00:05:34.370 --> 00:05:35.886
for any rock balancer.

00:05:35.886 --> 00:05:38.553
Either it collapses or it stays balanced.

00:05:38.553 --> 00:05:41.304
There's been so many times where

00:05:41.304 --> 00:05:42.418
I just wanted to quit.

00:05:42.418 --> 00:05:44.091
Like there was a storm coming in,

00:05:44.091 --> 00:05:45.873
if it was windy, it started raining,

00:05:45.873 --> 00:05:49.440
or it's snowing and my
fingers are freezing,

00:05:49.440 --> 00:05:52.141
but I just keep going
because I know it's possible.

00:05:52.141 --> 00:05:54.916
I visualized it, I felt
it, but it just takes those

00:05:54.916 --> 00:05:58.499
micro-adjustments and 95% of the time,

00:05:59.826 --> 00:06:02.736
it usually works out if I
stick with it long enough.

00:06:02.736 --> 00:06:07.042
And sticking with it long
enough can be a few days.

00:06:07.042 --> 00:06:08.530
It's like a yoga almost.

00:06:08.530 --> 00:06:10.593
It's like a pure deep meditation,

00:06:10.593 --> 00:06:12.919
and maybe that sounds a
little bit hokey pokey,

00:06:12.919 --> 00:06:14.919
but it's a real feeling.

00:06:23.654 --> 00:06:24.688
- [Narrator] The staggering views in space

00:06:24.688 --> 00:06:28.105
can play tricks on the eyes and the mind.

00:06:29.409 --> 00:06:32.159
Astronaut Leland Melvin explains.

00:06:34.663 --> 00:06:37.676
- As we went around the
planet 17,500 miles per hour

00:06:37.676 --> 00:06:40.086
we saw this green glow when you're in

00:06:40.086 --> 00:06:42.524
the southern hemisphere, and
in the northern hemisphere

00:06:42.524 --> 00:06:46.196
you would see these purple
and yellow and blue lights

00:06:46.196 --> 00:06:50.363
just popping from the particles
hitting the atmosphere.

00:06:52.858 --> 00:06:56.869
Every orbit, the sun rises
and sets every 45 minutes,

00:06:56.869 --> 00:06:58.348
so as you go around the planet,

00:06:58.348 --> 00:07:01.549
you see this setting and then
another 45 minutes later,

00:07:01.549 --> 00:07:03.382
the rising of the sun.

00:07:04.746 --> 00:07:06.504
On my first mission, I was told that

00:07:06.504 --> 00:07:08.747
cosmic rays would come through the vehicle

00:07:08.747 --> 00:07:11.286
and hit your optical nerve.

00:07:11.286 --> 00:07:14.036
It would make you think
you're seeing flashes of light

00:07:14.036 --> 00:07:16.263
even though that's not really happening.

00:07:16.263 --> 00:07:19.925
The flashes were like a sun
burst of different colors.

00:07:19.925 --> 00:07:23.245
You would see the streaks of color

00:07:23.245 --> 00:07:26.544
popping in your eyes and in your head.

00:07:26.544 --> 00:07:28.738
Sleeping in space is pretty incredible

00:07:28.738 --> 00:07:31.601
because you're floating
inside your sleeping bag,

00:07:31.601 --> 00:07:35.973
but then you had this whole
den of pumps and motors

00:07:35.973 --> 00:07:37.769
and things whizzing around you,

00:07:37.769 --> 00:07:39.631
and so it was almost like, uh,
you're in a factory

00:07:39.631 --> 00:07:43.798
in a mechanical kind of
sounding automation going on.

00:07:47.069 --> 00:07:49.872
My dreams were so vivid from
all the stimulus from the day.

00:07:49.872 --> 00:07:52.205
I saw blues, greens, whites,

00:07:54.344 --> 00:07:56.972
whether they came from the ocean,

00:07:56.972 --> 00:07:59.689
whether it came from the
sun, whether it came from

00:07:59.689 --> 00:08:03.400
flashes in my head from
these high energy particles.

00:08:03.400 --> 00:08:06.613
The colors were intertwined
into my dream-state,

00:08:06.613 --> 00:08:09.969
and I saw sometimes alien forms.

00:08:09.969 --> 00:08:14.152
The green aurora australis
is moving and dancing

00:08:14.152 --> 00:08:16.940
so you know, you think of
a little green man on mars.

00:08:16.940 --> 00:08:19.726
There was one point in the mission

00:08:19.726 --> 00:08:22.145
where I started dreaming
about an inside-out

00:08:22.145 --> 00:08:24.493
cheeseburger with the most grease

00:08:24.493 --> 00:08:27.280
and just chomping into this hamburger.

00:08:27.280 --> 00:08:28.941
It's something that we
cannot have in space.

00:08:28.941 --> 00:08:31.950
And also a big slice of pizza.

00:08:31.950 --> 00:08:34.319
I think because I've had these experiences

00:08:34.319 --> 00:08:37.779
and I’ve seen these incredible
things in the universe,

00:08:37.779 --> 00:08:39.877
all of that's now incorporated
into my dreams now.

00:08:39.877 --> 00:08:42.044
So I constantly dream about

00:08:42.044 --> 00:08:46.211
lights flashing and colors and
velvet pitch black night sky

00:08:47.815 --> 00:08:51.565
and these little white
lights coming through.

00:08:52.459 --> 00:08:54.065
My name is Leland Melvin,

00:08:54.065 --> 00:08:57.982
I'm an astronaut, and a
great big story teller.

00:08:59.903 --> 00:09:01.276
- [Narrator] At any given moment,

00:09:01.276 --> 00:09:03.272
there's more than a
million asteroids hurling

00:09:03.272 --> 00:09:05.507
through our solar system.

00:09:05.507 --> 00:09:08.924
Most will never come close to our planet.

00:09:10.200 --> 00:09:11.547
But NASA doesn't have the bandwidth

00:09:11.547 --> 00:09:14.223
to keep track of them all.

00:09:14.223 --> 00:09:15.365
That's where a small community

00:09:15.365 --> 00:09:17.865
of ordinary citizens steps in,

00:09:19.021 --> 00:09:21.118
backyard astronomers.

00:09:21.118 --> 00:09:22.626
Gary Hug is one of them.

00:09:22.626 --> 00:09:24.374
- [Gary] I really like to discover things

00:09:24.374 --> 00:09:26.975
that haven't been discovered yet.

00:09:26.975 --> 00:09:28.410
- [Narrator] A retired
machinist who turned

00:09:28.410 --> 00:09:31.530
his love of astronomy
into a full-time affair.

00:09:31.530 --> 00:09:32.859
- [Gary] If you discover an asteroid,

00:09:32.859 --> 00:09:34.344
that's one you found.

00:09:34.344 --> 00:09:36.967
Nobody else in the world
knew about that asteroid

00:09:36.967 --> 00:09:38.914
before you saw it.

00:09:38.914 --> 00:09:40.761
- [Narrator] He's discovered more than

00:09:40.761 --> 00:09:42.893
300 flaming balls of ice and rock,

00:09:42.893 --> 00:09:44.465
all by peering into a telescope

00:09:44.465 --> 00:09:46.965
he designed and built himself.

00:09:54.789 --> 00:09:56.134
- What I've been doing here lately

00:09:56.134 --> 00:09:58.717
and for about 15, 16 years now,

00:10:00.140 --> 00:10:03.110
I've been working with near-earth objects.

00:10:03.110 --> 00:10:06.720
My observations contribute to making sure

00:10:06.720 --> 00:10:08.790
that we don't have something coming up

00:10:08.790 --> 00:10:11.540
that's going to fall on our head.

00:10:13.978 --> 00:10:17.319
I kind of first got into it
probably 10, 12 years old.

00:10:17.319 --> 00:10:19.394
I was begging, begging,
begging to get a telescope,

00:10:19.394 --> 00:10:21.992
but you know, we weren't
a very rich family,

00:10:21.992 --> 00:10:23.452
so it took a while to get it.

00:10:23.452 --> 00:10:24.849
One Christmas I got telescope.

00:10:24.849 --> 00:10:27.917
Then I started using it, and
then I got aperture fever.

00:10:27.917 --> 00:10:31.407
You want bigger telescopes all the time,

00:10:31.407 --> 00:10:34.740
so I started learning how to build them.

00:10:36.408 --> 00:10:39.741
I learned how to work in a machine shop,

00:10:40.828 --> 00:10:44.661
and I can build things
that I wanted to build.

00:10:45.999 --> 00:10:49.073
And a lot of that was astronomy parts.

00:10:49.073 --> 00:10:53.076
I kind of modified my
life to fit this study,

00:10:53.076 --> 00:10:55.493
this kind of life-long study.

00:11:01.199 --> 00:11:05.245
(clock loudly ticking)

00:11:05.245 --> 00:11:08.477
Sandlot is a very tiny little observatory.

00:11:08.477 --> 00:11:10.920
It's only 10 foot by 10 foot wide.

00:11:10.920 --> 00:11:13.643
The telescope I built over a period

00:11:13.643 --> 00:11:14.902
of about a year and a half,

00:11:14.902 --> 00:11:18.295
and it may not look very polished.

00:11:18.295 --> 00:11:20.816
It works really well.

00:11:20.816 --> 00:11:22.399
I'm good with that.

00:11:26.754 --> 00:11:29.671
(electronic music)

00:11:59.529 --> 00:12:03.029
(camera shutter clicking)

00:12:20.325 --> 00:12:23.242
I don't have a degree in astronomy.

00:12:24.827 --> 00:12:27.445
I have a passion for astronomy,

00:12:27.445 --> 00:12:29.231
and I've done it for many, many years,

00:12:29.231 --> 00:12:31.429
and I've contributed, I think,

00:12:31.429 --> 00:12:33.870
to the scientific body of astronomy.

00:12:33.870 --> 00:12:37.413
That's really kind of a,
yeah, from that standpoint,

00:12:37.413 --> 00:12:38.996
I am an astronomer.

00:12:44.153 --> 00:12:46.187
(mysterious music)

00:12:46.187 --> 00:12:50.104
(speaking in foreign language)

00:15:01.117 --> 00:15:02.827
- So when you buy from local guys,

00:15:02.827 --> 00:15:04.635
it comes back to you.

00:15:04.635 --> 00:15:06.525
And you know you're getting a local fish,

00:15:06.525 --> 00:15:09.942
it's sustainable, you know, that's an honest thing.

00:15:13.466 --> 00:15:14.299
Thank you.

00:15:21.237 --> 00:15:22.070
- [Antonio] Sometimes we forget that

00:15:22.070 --> 00:15:24.566
we are part of the ecosystem.

00:15:24.566 --> 00:15:26.383
We are animals, too,

00:15:26.383 --> 00:15:28.868
and are very ignorant.

00:15:28.868 --> 00:15:30.035
Very ignorant.

00:16:02.394 --> 00:16:06.219
(piano notes ringing)

00:16:06.219 --> 00:16:08.490
- I've always been inspired
or fascinated by weather.

00:16:08.490 --> 00:16:11.082
What does a cloud sound like?

00:16:11.082 --> 00:16:12.791
You know, even as a
child I can remember sitting

00:16:12.791 --> 00:16:14.970
down on the grass and
looking up at the clouds

00:16:14.970 --> 00:16:17.101
as they go past.

00:16:17.101 --> 00:16:19.607
What kind of sound would
those clouds output

00:16:19.607 --> 00:16:23.190
using a piano if they, if they
roll across the keys?

00:16:27.396 --> 00:16:28.584
My name is David Bowen.

00:16:28.584 --> 00:16:31.317
I'm a studio artist and educator.

00:16:31.317 --> 00:16:33.896
I make kinetic and robotic sculpture,

00:16:33.896 --> 00:16:35.731
and often these robotic devices

00:16:35.731 --> 00:16:38.898
respond to some sort of natural input.

00:16:41.273 --> 00:16:43.186
Cloud piano is a robotic installation

00:16:43.186 --> 00:16:45.910
that plays a piano based on the movement

00:16:45.910 --> 00:16:48.949
and density of the clouds
as they move across the sky.

00:16:48.949 --> 00:16:50.550
As you can see, it's a device that mounts

00:16:50.550 --> 00:16:52.850
to the keyboard of a piano,

00:16:52.850 --> 00:16:55.020
and there's another component, a camera,

00:16:55.020 --> 00:16:57.494
that's mounted outside the building here,

00:16:57.494 --> 00:17:00.169
and as clouds go past the camera,

00:17:00.169 --> 00:17:02.415
the video is sent in
real time to a computer

00:17:02.415 --> 00:17:04.690
that then scales the data.

00:17:04.690 --> 00:17:07.667
What I've done is taken the
video feed from the camera

00:17:07.667 --> 00:17:10.763
and chopped it into 88 individual pieces,

00:17:10.763 --> 00:17:13.784
as if they are the 88
keys on the keyboard.

00:17:13.784 --> 00:17:16.421
So as the clouds go past a particular key,

00:17:16.421 --> 00:17:18.013
that key is pressed.

00:17:18.013 --> 00:17:20.066
And so it's basically a
simple algorithm, I suppose,

00:17:20.066 --> 00:17:23.427
that says if you see more white than blue

00:17:23.427 --> 00:17:25.871
in this little section of the video,

00:17:25.871 --> 00:17:27.538
then press that key.

00:17:28.652 --> 00:17:30.502
I guess you could think of it then as

00:17:30.502 --> 00:17:34.313
maybe a collaboration where I
set up this certain situation

00:17:34.313 --> 00:17:37.148
and I'm making these
choices, but after that

00:17:37.148 --> 00:17:39.731
it's kind of up to the weather.

00:17:41.690 --> 00:17:45.112
These odd sounds that the piano is making

00:17:45.112 --> 00:17:47.673
will continue to build or dissipate

00:17:47.673 --> 00:17:49.840
or move and change shape.

00:17:51.089 --> 00:17:53.014
Machines or computers are thought to be

00:17:53.014 --> 00:17:55.288
very orderly or very precise and likewise,

00:17:55.288 --> 00:17:58.443
nature is thought to be very chaotic.

00:17:58.443 --> 00:18:00.848
What keeps me interested
is I think there is

00:18:00.848 --> 00:18:03.300
a lot of systematic ways
to look at these things

00:18:03.300 --> 00:18:05.736
that we associate with chaos.

00:18:05.736 --> 00:18:08.903
(piano notes ringing)

00:18:24.167 --> 00:18:25.771
- [Narrator] Every year, the world's

00:18:25.771 --> 00:18:28.207
most elite mountain
bikers gather in France

00:18:28.207 --> 00:18:30.468
for a race unlike any other.

00:18:30.468 --> 00:18:34.385
(speaking in foreign language)

00:18:38.612 --> 00:18:42.379
- [Narrator] Megavalanche is
18 miles long, to be exact,

00:18:42.379 --> 00:18:45.961
and it's an adrenaline filled
ride through all four seasons,

00:18:45.961 --> 00:18:49.321
starting in an unlikely
spot for a bike race,

00:18:49.321 --> 00:18:52.061
11,000 feet up on the snow capped summit

00:18:52.061 --> 00:18:54.728
of Pic Blanc in the French Alps.

00:18:56.138 --> 00:18:58.149
This is Remy Absalon.

00:18:58.149 --> 00:19:00.854
He won the Megavalanche.

00:19:00.854 --> 00:19:03.318
- I start mountain bike when I was young.

00:19:03.318 --> 00:19:06.270
Enduro mountain bike is really cool

00:19:06.270 --> 00:19:09.937
because we can explore
all around the world.

00:19:11.877 --> 00:19:13.416
Megavalanche Alpe D'Huez take place

00:19:13.416 --> 00:19:16.877
in the French Alps in Alpe D'Huez,

00:19:16.877 --> 00:19:20.127
so not so far of Italy and Switzerland.

00:19:21.216 --> 00:19:24.060
It's really high in the Alps,

00:19:24.060 --> 00:19:27.132
so you have a very good view,

00:19:27.132 --> 00:19:29.049
360 degrees around you.

00:19:31.957 --> 00:19:33.035
It's really crazy.

00:19:33.035 --> 00:19:35.285
I don't know why I do that.

00:19:40.507 --> 00:19:42.490
We ride all kind of terrain.

00:19:42.490 --> 00:19:46.657
First the glassy and after the
rocky sections, the forest.

00:19:51.052 --> 00:19:54.496
It's very physical with some hairpins,

00:19:54.496 --> 00:19:58.015
so when you cross the
line and you are first,

00:19:58.015 --> 00:20:02.333
you are really happy, and
you want to try again.

00:20:02.333 --> 00:20:06.500
(announcer yelling in foreign language)

00:20:07.463 --> 00:20:11.546
(audience clapping and cheering)

00:20:14.888 --> 00:20:16.535
- [Narrator] About a
third of the United States

00:20:16.535 --> 00:20:19.185
is covered in forests.

00:20:19.185 --> 00:20:20.787
In places like Colorado,

00:20:20.787 --> 00:20:23.120
those forests are in danger.

00:20:24.312 --> 00:20:25.145
There's fire.

00:20:25.145 --> 00:20:26.772
- [Gretchen] Our wildfires are getting

00:20:26.772 --> 00:20:28.701
more frequent and larger.

00:20:28.701 --> 00:20:30.673
- [Narrator] A devastating
beetle infestation.

00:20:30.673 --> 00:20:32.104
- Those trees are all dead.

00:20:32.104 --> 00:20:33.392
They don't have any branches anymore.

00:20:33.392 --> 00:20:35.464
They're just standing sticks.

00:20:35.464 --> 00:20:37.413
- [Narrator] And global warming.

00:20:37.413 --> 00:20:39.065
There is hope,

00:20:39.065 --> 00:20:42.156
and it's found in an unlikely place.

00:20:42.156 --> 00:20:43.924
- When I tell people that I work

00:20:43.924 --> 00:20:46.377
at a forest in Nebraska,
they usually laugh,

00:20:46.377 --> 00:20:49.877
and they're like, "Nebraska has a forest?"

00:20:55.538 --> 00:20:58.883
- [Narrator] Richard
Gilbert is a man on a quest.

00:20:58.883 --> 00:21:02.771
He's a biological
scientist who works here,

00:21:02.771 --> 00:21:05.747
at the Charles Bessey
Nursery in Halsey, Nebraska.

00:21:05.747 --> 00:21:10.226
It's the oldest federal
tree nursery in the U.S.

00:21:10.226 --> 00:21:12.376
The nursery's main mission
is to help preserve

00:21:12.376 --> 00:21:14.515
and repopulate national forests

00:21:14.515 --> 00:21:17.254
in the Rocky Mountain region.

00:21:17.254 --> 00:21:19.434
- Without Bessey, they're
not going to be able to have

00:21:19.434 --> 00:21:23.841
the supply of trees that
they need for reforestation.

00:21:23.841 --> 00:21:28.008
If they have a catastrophe,
we can be there to help them.

00:21:34.670 --> 00:21:37.693
You know, I think most of
the perception for Nebraska

00:21:37.693 --> 00:21:40.245
is that it's large and it's flat.

00:21:40.245 --> 00:21:42.456
Very boring, not pretty at all.

00:21:42.456 --> 00:21:45.348
Lots of corn and soybeans.

00:21:45.348 --> 00:21:47.526
But there's lots of beauty here.

00:21:47.526 --> 00:21:48.584
There really is.

00:21:48.584 --> 00:21:51.506
Nebraska had been treeless, 1800s,

00:21:51.506 --> 00:21:54.870
it has been treeless for quite some time.

00:21:54.870 --> 00:21:57.543
Charles E. Bessey was a botany professor

00:21:57.543 --> 00:21:59.627
at the University of Nebraska.

00:21:59.627 --> 00:22:02.894
Absolutely loved Nebraska,
loved the sand hills,

00:22:02.894 --> 00:22:04.735
he loved trees.

00:22:04.735 --> 00:22:08.422
His mission was to get a forest planted

00:22:08.422 --> 00:22:10.255
somewhere in Nebraska.

00:22:15.635 --> 00:22:17.407
They got seedlings out of the Black Hills.

00:22:17.407 --> 00:22:20.407
They got seedlings out of Minnesota.

00:22:24.358 --> 00:22:26.845
And they brought them
here and planted them

00:22:26.845 --> 00:22:28.967
onto the forest.

00:22:28.967 --> 00:22:32.134
- 20,000 acres were hand planted here.

00:22:36.355 --> 00:22:39.059
- [Richard] It was chosen
for the plentiful water,

00:22:39.059 --> 00:22:41.723
the sand, and the ease of
producing the seedlings

00:22:41.723 --> 00:22:43.954
and extracting them out of the soil,

00:22:43.954 --> 00:22:46.029
getting them bundled
up, and planted back out

00:22:46.029 --> 00:22:48.446
onto the forest very quickly.

00:22:56.869 --> 00:22:58.704
What I love most about my job

00:22:58.704 --> 00:23:01.505
is really the change in season,

00:23:01.505 --> 00:23:03.820
and the change in work that I do.

00:23:03.820 --> 00:23:05.844
I get to work in the seed bank,

00:23:05.844 --> 00:23:08.100
I get to work in the
container part of this,

00:23:08.100 --> 00:23:09.631
and I get to work in the field.

00:23:09.631 --> 00:23:12.440
And I get to actually get my hands dirty.

00:23:12.440 --> 00:23:16.801
I don't just sit in an
office and push paper.

00:23:16.801 --> 00:23:17.933
- Richard's amazing.

00:23:17.933 --> 00:23:22.100
He really cares, and he's
always trying to make it better.

00:23:25.287 --> 00:23:26.636
- We turn the greenhouses on.

00:23:26.636 --> 00:23:28.796
You got little seedlings germinating.

00:23:28.796 --> 00:23:30.813
To me, it's spring.

00:23:30.813 --> 00:23:34.066
So my spring starts really early,

00:23:34.066 --> 00:23:36.483
and it's extremely rewarding.

00:23:37.442 --> 00:23:38.923
- He's very proud of his work.

00:23:38.923 --> 00:23:41.404
He's very proud of his trees.

00:23:41.404 --> 00:23:43.965
- [Richard] I love growing
plants and helping them grow.

00:23:43.965 --> 00:23:48.132
I don't ever want to lose
that contact with the plants.

00:23:52.434 --> 00:23:54.475
- The San Juan National
Forest I think is about

00:23:54.475 --> 00:23:55.975
1.6 million acres.

00:23:57.365 --> 00:23:59.726
One of our biggest challenges
that's happening is

00:23:59.726 --> 00:24:02.669
we have a large epidemic
of spruce beetle happening

00:24:02.669 --> 00:24:05.336
on the San Juan National Forest.

00:24:12.267 --> 00:24:13.554
We believe that the beetle epidemics

00:24:13.554 --> 00:24:16.257
were controlled by cold winters.

00:24:16.257 --> 00:24:18.062
Climate change is happening,

00:24:18.062 --> 00:24:21.104
and we know up here that we
have not had cold winters.

00:24:21.104 --> 00:24:25.104
Cold winters is what
controls beetle epidemics.

00:24:37.730 --> 00:24:39.222
Where we're seeing the spruce
beetle attacking the trees,

00:24:39.222 --> 00:24:41.421
when those trees are all dead,

00:24:41.421 --> 00:24:42.786
they don't have any branches anymore,

00:24:42.786 --> 00:24:44.240
they're just standing sticks.

00:24:44.240 --> 00:24:46.740
They're starting to fall over.

00:24:52.280 --> 00:24:53.710
The spruce beetle epidemic

00:24:53.710 --> 00:24:56.147
that we're seeing is unprecedented.

00:24:56.147 --> 00:24:59.314
We collect seed from the trees early

00:25:00.279 --> 00:25:03.420
before the beetles hit
to maintain a seed bank.

00:25:03.420 --> 00:25:06.476
Bessey Nursery is a very important
part of our organization.

00:25:06.476 --> 00:25:09.341
They are the ones who
store all of our seed,

00:25:09.341 --> 00:25:12.591
and they grow all the seedlings for us.

00:25:16.248 --> 00:25:18.008
- When they need ‘em, we grow ‘em,

00:25:18.008 --> 00:25:22.548
and we get ‘em produced
in less than a year.

00:25:22.548 --> 00:25:25.204
All of the trees are
produced in the greenhouses,

00:25:25.204 --> 00:25:27.694
and then they are brought
in here on tables.

00:25:27.694 --> 00:25:30.768
This wire's okay, because
it's above halfway,

00:25:30.768 --> 00:25:33.442
and we have a central leader.

00:25:33.442 --> 00:25:37.609
We can pack 110 to 120 thousand
trees per belt per day.

00:25:40.476 --> 00:25:42.208
We're packing container trees for

00:25:42.208 --> 00:25:45.376
the San Juan National Forest.

00:25:45.376 --> 00:25:46.292
They don't get their moisture

00:25:46.292 --> 00:25:47.781
in the spring a lot of times.

00:25:47.781 --> 00:25:50.093
They get their summer monsoon seasons.

00:25:50.093 --> 00:25:52.659
That's when they want
their seedlings delivered.

00:25:52.659 --> 00:25:55.591
- Richard remembers all
of this information.

00:25:55.591 --> 00:25:58.144
He wants the feedback and
wants to know how they did.

00:25:58.144 --> 00:26:02.727
At our best, we might
get 70%, 80% survival.

00:26:02.727 --> 00:26:04.208
- [Richard] Without
Bessey, they're not going

00:26:04.208 --> 00:26:06.509
to be able to have the supply of trees

00:26:06.509 --> 00:26:09.259
that they need for reforestation.

00:26:20.618 --> 00:26:23.817
- Rich Gilbert is very
proactive, and he's adapting,

00:26:23.817 --> 00:26:25.977
and doing as much as he can

00:26:25.977 --> 00:26:28.745
to prepare the nursery into the future.

00:26:28.745 --> 00:26:32.126
- [Richard] Starting from the
seed to a finished product,

00:26:32.126 --> 00:26:33.610
and it's a wonderful experience

00:26:33.610 --> 00:26:35.792
to be able to be part of that.

00:26:35.792 --> 00:26:37.694
- [Man] These trees are
going to outlast me,

00:26:37.694 --> 00:26:39.121
and they're going to outlast Rich.

00:26:39.121 --> 00:26:40.515
It's not often you can have that

00:26:40.515 --> 00:26:43.182
kind of legacy on the landscape.

00:26:44.061 --> 00:26:45.512
- Plants are just amazing,

00:26:45.512 --> 00:26:47.493
and there are so many of them out there,

00:26:47.493 --> 00:26:50.037
and I will never know how
to grow every single one.

00:26:50.037 --> 00:26:52.327
If it's a very, very interesting plant,

00:26:52.327 --> 00:26:55.116
I'll try to produce it
and grow it at the house,

00:26:55.116 --> 00:26:56.728
and if you guys drive
by my house over there,

00:26:56.728 --> 00:26:58.075
you'll understand.

00:26:58.075 --> 00:27:02.242
You'll see all the different
plants that I have over there.

00:27:06.851 --> 00:27:08.726
- [Narrator] Across
the planet, coral reefs

00:27:08.726 --> 00:27:11.215
are dying at an alarming rate.

00:27:11.215 --> 00:27:14.424
Scientists estimate that
30% of the ocean's reefs

00:27:14.424 --> 00:27:17.747
have disappeared due to human activity

00:27:17.747 --> 00:27:20.090
and rising ocean temperatures.

00:27:20.090 --> 00:27:21.790
- Coral is kind of like
the trees of a forest.

00:27:21.790 --> 00:27:25.301
They're just the backbone of
a whole tropical ecosystem,

00:27:25.301 --> 00:27:29.312
and if they disappear,
we're in a lot of trouble.

00:27:29.312 --> 00:27:32.672
- [Narrator] Ken Nedimyer
is coral's last best hope.

00:27:32.672 --> 00:27:35.849
He developed the coral tree nursery,

00:27:35.849 --> 00:27:40.327
a simple framework of PVC pipe
tethered to the ocean floor.

00:27:40.327 --> 00:27:42.932
In this nursery, he's able
to grow brand new fragments

00:27:42.932 --> 00:27:45.375
of elkhorn and staghorn coral

00:27:45.375 --> 00:27:49.803
and replant them on struggling
reefs in the Florida Keys.

00:27:49.803 --> 00:27:52.667
He's planting 25,000 new corals a year,

00:27:52.667 --> 00:27:54.788
and his methods are
inspiring conservationists

00:27:54.788 --> 00:27:56.371
across the tropics.

00:28:04.013 --> 00:28:05.502
- Been growing up diving.

00:28:05.502 --> 00:28:06.502
Spent a lot of time in the water.

00:28:06.502 --> 00:28:07.835
One, two, three.

00:28:10.042 --> 00:28:12.252
And I've basically watched
the coral reefs dying.

00:28:12.252 --> 00:28:14.664
As the reefs died, the
fish didn't come back,

00:28:14.664 --> 00:28:16.366
and I realized, I'm tired
of watching it die,

00:28:16.366 --> 00:28:18.386
I need to do something about it;

00:28:18.386 --> 00:28:20.839
and so I developed this
whole idea of growing corals

00:28:20.839 --> 00:28:25.006
in an offshore nursery and
replanting them on the reef.

00:28:26.767 --> 00:28:28.476
- [Man] So you're kind of like a farmer?

00:28:28.476 --> 00:28:29.726
- I'm a farmer.

00:28:30.853 --> 00:28:32.767
Yeah, we grow corals just like a farm.

00:28:32.767 --> 00:28:34.125
There's a good time of the year to plant

00:28:34.125 --> 00:28:35.872
and there's a harvest time.

00:28:35.872 --> 00:28:38.597
We have five offshore
nurseries in the Keys.

00:28:38.597 --> 00:28:41.097
Each tree can hold 100 corals.

00:28:42.322 --> 00:28:44.346
We start with little
fragments that we collect,

00:28:44.346 --> 00:28:46.865
and after six to nine
months, maybe a year,

00:28:46.865 --> 00:28:49.920
that fragment has turned
into a colony that might have

00:28:49.920 --> 00:28:52.113
100 centimeters of growth on it,

00:28:52.113 --> 00:28:55.350
we cut that off, and then
we plant it out on the reef.

00:28:55.350 --> 00:28:57.149
So we just harvested some corals,

00:28:57.149 --> 00:28:59.502
and we're going to take
them out to Pickle's Reef

00:28:59.502 --> 00:29:01.312
right now and plant them.

00:29:01.312 --> 00:29:02.908
We've got a couple
thousand of them out there

00:29:02.908 --> 00:29:03.747
that we've already planted,

00:29:03.747 --> 00:29:06.975
and we're going to add some more.

00:29:06.975 --> 00:29:08.566
It is a bit of gardening when you put

00:29:08.566 --> 00:29:10.009
the corals back out on the reef,

00:29:10.009 --> 00:29:11.803
you're saying ah, I think that
would look nice over there,

00:29:11.803 --> 00:29:13.670
and that would look nice over there,

00:29:13.670 --> 00:29:15.563
and some of it's based
on what used to be there

00:29:15.563 --> 00:29:20.155
and what should be there, and
that's how we do our planting.

00:29:20.155 --> 00:29:22.519
We planted 20,000 already this year.

00:29:22.519 --> 00:29:24.470
We'll plant 25,000 next year.

00:29:24.470 --> 00:29:26.288
Those corals will
probably spawn next year.

00:29:26.288 --> 00:29:29.041
Part of the long-range
goal is to get the corals

00:29:29.041 --> 00:29:31.698
reproducing on their own.

00:29:31.698 --> 00:29:33.454
I'm excited every time I get in the water,

00:29:33.454 --> 00:29:34.969
whether I'm going to work in the nursery

00:29:34.969 --> 00:29:36.604
or whether I'm planting corals

00:29:36.604 --> 00:29:39.890
or just looking for new areas to plant.

00:29:39.890 --> 00:29:41.429
A lot of people said, “Oh,
you can never do that.”

00:29:41.429 --> 00:29:43.287
Can't do it on a big enough scale,

00:29:43.287 --> 00:29:45.140
and I think I've proven
that it can be done,

00:29:45.140 --> 00:29:47.396
and if we can train enough people

00:29:47.396 --> 00:29:49.947
and teach enough people in other places,

00:29:49.947 --> 00:29:52.638
I think we can really see
a significant turn-around.

00:29:52.638 --> 00:29:54.805
Another day, another coral.

00:30:01.547 --> 00:30:04.783
- [Narrator] The world's
oceans are under siege.

00:30:04.783 --> 00:30:07.099
Marine life is dying at alarming rates

00:30:07.099 --> 00:30:09.512
due to accelerating
pollution, overfishing,

00:30:09.512 --> 00:30:10.878
and climate change.

00:30:10.878 --> 00:30:13.138
- People keep saying
we're at a tipping point.

00:30:13.138 --> 00:30:15.813
Some people say we're
beyond a tipping point.

00:30:15.813 --> 00:30:18.859
- [Narrator] But six miles
off the shores of Key Largo,

00:30:18.859 --> 00:30:20.121
and 60 feet deep

00:30:20.121 --> 00:30:23.278
sits Aquarius, the world's only

00:30:23.278 --> 00:30:26.251
underwater research laboratory.

00:30:26.251 --> 00:30:28.774
- There is no other place
where people live underwater

00:30:28.774 --> 00:30:32.259
and work on the water for
extended periods of time.

00:30:32.259 --> 00:30:34.504
- [Narrator] The
diver-scientists who work here

00:30:34.504 --> 00:30:36.587
are called aquanauts,

00:30:36.587 --> 00:30:38.301
and they come from all over the world

00:30:38.301 --> 00:30:40.583
to spend weeks at Florida
International University's

00:30:40.583 --> 00:30:41.833
underwater lab,

00:30:43.063 --> 00:30:47.334
conducting cutting-edge
research on oceanic health.

00:30:47.334 --> 00:30:48.895
This elite group faces
danger in the elements

00:30:48.895 --> 00:30:53.062
for the sake of the planet
in a race against time.

00:30:56.892 --> 00:30:58.465
- The first time I saw an ocean,

00:30:58.465 --> 00:31:01.400
I was amazed by the vast expanse.

00:31:01.400 --> 00:31:04.483
It really made me feel insignificant.

00:31:06.067 --> 00:31:10.259
As I started to spend more and
more time around the ocean,

00:31:10.259 --> 00:31:12.751
I started to see that we do

00:31:12.751 --> 00:31:15.668
affect what's happening underwater.

00:31:16.909 --> 00:31:19.058
We're seeing ocean acidification.

00:31:19.058 --> 00:31:21.807
We're seeing coral reef decline.

00:31:21.807 --> 00:31:24.474
And we see a lot of degradation.

00:31:36.958 --> 00:31:39.476
What I don't think people realize is

00:31:39.476 --> 00:31:42.383
the impact that oceans have on people

00:31:42.383 --> 00:31:44.454
in the middle of the country.

00:31:44.454 --> 00:31:48.271
Everything associated with
the ocean controls climate,

00:31:48.271 --> 00:31:51.271
controls local weatherized patterns.

00:31:57.086 --> 00:31:58.505
- The world's reefs have been valued

00:31:58.505 --> 00:32:00.588
at $375 billion annually.

00:32:01.625 --> 00:32:03.835
That's for fisheries, tourism,

00:32:03.835 --> 00:32:07.381
protection of the coastline from storms.

00:32:07.381 --> 00:32:09.211
- The research at Aquarius
is being conducted

00:32:09.211 --> 00:32:10.794
at a critical time.

00:32:12.279 --> 00:32:13.919
Aquarius, Aquarius Reef Base.

00:32:13.919 --> 00:32:16.463
- Got camera feeds and all that, right?

00:32:16.463 --> 00:32:18.107
- That's a roger, we've
got the main lock on.

00:32:18.107 --> 00:32:20.869
Aquarius is the last
undersea research laboratory

00:32:20.869 --> 00:32:25.107
dedicated to science and
eduction in the world today.

00:32:25.107 --> 00:32:27.583
- There is no other underwater habitat

00:32:27.583 --> 00:32:30.142
doing what we do, how we do it.

00:32:30.142 --> 00:32:31.771
- [Anthony] It allows researchers

00:32:31.771 --> 00:32:33.647
to live at their study site.

00:32:33.647 --> 00:32:36.462
They can do incredibly long working dives,

00:32:36.462 --> 00:32:40.295
dives which couldn't be
done from the surface.

00:32:42.383 --> 00:32:44.304
- When you're diving from the surface,

00:32:44.304 --> 00:32:46.665
the deeper you go, the less time you have.

00:32:46.665 --> 00:32:50.507
When you live underwater, the habitat

00:32:50.507 --> 00:32:53.442
actually becomes your surface.

00:32:53.442 --> 00:32:57.797
When you dive from the habitat,
which is about 50 feet,

00:32:57.797 --> 00:32:59.734
and you dive to 90 feet,

00:32:59.734 --> 00:33:02.887
you're not really diving to 90 feet,

00:33:02.887 --> 00:33:06.118
you're really diving to
40 feet from the surface.

00:33:06.118 --> 00:33:09.562
So now what you've done is
you've extended that time period

00:33:09.562 --> 00:33:12.097
that you can actually spend at 90 feet.

00:33:12.097 --> 00:33:14.840
That's where it really
separates from the traditional

00:33:14.840 --> 00:33:17.016
research or work that's done from vessels

00:33:17.016 --> 00:33:19.247
on the surface of the water.

00:33:19.247 --> 00:33:20.944
That makes a big difference.

00:33:20.944 --> 00:33:24.196
- Marine ecologists use
it as a base of operation.

00:33:24.196 --> 00:33:25.845
Others want to use it for

00:33:25.845 --> 00:33:28.127
extreme environment mission operations.

00:33:28.127 --> 00:33:31.484
We do a number of projects with NASA,

00:33:31.484 --> 00:33:34.569
and they say that Aquarius is very similar

00:33:34.569 --> 00:33:37.819
to what astronauts experience in space.

00:33:41.486 --> 00:33:45.216
In the ‘50s, ‘60s, and ‘70s, we had 60 or 70

00:33:45.216 --> 00:33:48.822
research labs underwater,
and now we're down to one.

00:33:48.822 --> 00:33:51.652
In 2011, there was a
shift within congress,

00:33:51.652 --> 00:33:55.234
so the National Oceanic and
Atmospheric Administration

00:33:55.234 --> 00:33:57.617
started to pull back in funding.

00:33:57.617 --> 00:33:59.701
Everyone in the science community said

00:33:59.701 --> 00:34:01.785
you know what, we've got to
do what we can to save this.

00:34:01.785 --> 00:34:04.202
FIU leapt at the opportunity.

00:34:05.950 --> 00:34:07.186
- It makes it official.

00:34:07.186 --> 00:34:10.019
The FIU age of Aquarius has begun.

00:34:11.388 --> 00:34:13.882
- [Thomas] We had gone from
getting ready to shut it down.

00:34:13.882 --> 00:34:16.296
- And here we are almost four years later,

00:34:16.296 --> 00:34:18.129
and we're still going.

00:34:23.443 --> 00:34:25.103
- [Thomas] You can
control a lot of things,

00:34:25.103 --> 00:34:27.005
but you can't control the weather.

00:34:27.005 --> 00:34:29.766
- [Roger] We make a choice
to take on the inherent risks

00:34:29.766 --> 00:34:33.933
that are associated with
being exposed to the elements.

00:34:35.309 --> 00:34:38.059
The constant real-time changes
that occur with weather,

00:34:38.059 --> 00:34:41.976
the possibility of getting
stranded out at sea.

00:34:43.091 --> 00:34:44.230
- [Narrator] The surface crew is forced

00:34:44.230 --> 00:34:45.920
to postpone operations.

00:34:45.920 --> 00:34:47.735
- Right now, it's not really a good idea

00:34:47.735 --> 00:34:49.205
to have divers in the water,

00:34:49.205 --> 00:34:52.536
especially with a thunderstorm
that has lightning strikes.

00:34:52.536 --> 00:34:53.844
- [Narrator] But the aquanauts below

00:34:53.844 --> 00:34:56.761
continue their work, uninterrupted.

00:34:57.893 --> 00:35:00.296
- Everybody who works in
an offshore environment

00:35:00.296 --> 00:35:04.463
fully understands that that
mission may be postponed a week,

00:35:05.497 --> 00:35:08.580
a month, maybe cancelled at any time.

00:35:16.401 --> 00:35:17.723
- Everybody when they're five years old

00:35:17.723 --> 00:35:19.718
wants to be an astronaut
or a marine biologist.

00:35:19.718 --> 00:35:21.068
I never grew out of it.

00:35:21.068 --> 00:35:22.951
Living and doing science underwater

00:35:22.951 --> 00:35:25.483
is the coolest thing I could possibly imagine.

00:35:25.483 --> 00:35:27.445
- What does it take to become an aquanaut?

00:35:27.445 --> 00:35:29.239
We look at people who have

00:35:29.239 --> 00:35:31.469
considerable experience underwater.

00:35:31.469 --> 00:35:33.227
Diving has to be second-nature.

00:35:33.227 --> 00:35:34.648
And it's not uncommon for people

00:35:34.648 --> 00:35:37.123
not to finish aquanaut training.

00:35:37.123 --> 00:35:40.284
They start to understand
that maybe psychologically,

00:35:40.284 --> 00:35:43.604
I'm not prepared to be
in a confined environment

00:35:43.604 --> 00:35:46.387
with five other people for 10 days.

00:35:46.387 --> 00:35:48.542
- That's the typical length of missions,

00:35:48.542 --> 00:35:50.804
but we have done longer ones.

00:35:50.804 --> 00:35:53.495
We've done 16 day, we've done 18 day,

00:35:53.495 --> 00:35:57.381
and the longest one to-date
had been 31 days long.

00:35:57.381 --> 00:36:00.464
- We've saturated 392 scientists now.

00:36:02.829 --> 00:36:04.001
- Both you guys got your masks, fins, BC,

00:36:04.001 --> 00:36:06.289
depth gauge, pressure gauge, computer?

00:36:06.289 --> 00:36:07.122
- Check.

00:36:07.122 --> 00:36:09.143
- I can honestly tell you
from the bottom of my heart

00:36:09.143 --> 00:36:11.753
that I enjoy waking up in the morning

00:36:11.753 --> 00:36:13.575
and then coming to work.

00:36:13.575 --> 00:36:15.978
Yeah, I enjoy my job, a lot.

00:36:15.978 --> 00:36:17.653
- It's not an easy job,

00:36:17.653 --> 00:36:21.464
keeping Aquarius going,
constantly fighting weather,

00:36:21.464 --> 00:36:23.708
constantly fighting corrosion,

00:36:23.708 --> 00:36:26.795
but every day we wake
up and say, all right,

00:36:26.795 --> 00:36:29.142
we've got to do something today,

00:36:29.142 --> 00:36:33.309
because what's happening with
the oceans isn't stopping.

00:36:44.047 --> 00:36:45.634
- [Narrator] This team of Hawaiian sailors

00:36:45.634 --> 00:36:49.134
is on what might sound
like an impossible mission,

00:36:49.134 --> 00:36:50.833
to sail around the world using only

00:36:50.833 --> 00:36:53.916
the sun and the stars as their guide.

00:36:56.521 --> 00:36:58.383
Their boat is a replica of

00:36:58.383 --> 00:37:00.891
a historic Polynesian voyaging canoe.

00:37:00.891 --> 00:37:03.058
She's called the Hokule'a.

00:37:04.168 --> 00:37:08.352
There's no motor, no nails,
no metal to hold her together.

00:37:08.352 --> 00:37:09.269
Just ropes.

00:37:11.886 --> 00:37:14.925
The goal for Captain Bruce
Blankenfeld and his crew?

00:37:14.925 --> 00:37:17.960
To honor their ancestors who
used this form of navigation,

00:37:17.960 --> 00:37:21.366
and prove it is possible
to sail the open seas

00:37:21.366 --> 00:37:23.866
without any modern technology.

00:37:29.948 --> 00:37:32.622
- Voyaging canoes, the
mode of transportation

00:37:32.622 --> 00:37:34.825
for navigating the vast

00:37:34.825 --> 00:37:36.325
area of Polynesia.

00:37:38.245 --> 00:37:41.657
It's like over 3 million square miles,

00:37:41.657 --> 00:37:45.652
and that was the way
our ancestors traveled.

00:37:45.652 --> 00:37:49.652
This is our culture, and
we're celebrating that.

00:37:51.082 --> 00:37:52.790
So we're on Hokule'a.

00:37:52.790 --> 00:37:56.707
She is a replica of a
Polynesian voyaging canoe.

00:37:58.990 --> 00:38:02.429
The Hokule'a was built
with the express purpose

00:38:02.429 --> 00:38:05.698
of proving that navigation
by the ancient way

00:38:05.698 --> 00:38:07.093
was very viable.

00:38:07.093 --> 00:38:09.346
That these canoes could be guided

00:38:09.346 --> 00:38:12.346
over 2,000 miles and long distances.

00:38:15.594 --> 00:38:18.058
- There's about five miles
of rope on this canoe,

00:38:18.058 --> 00:38:19.895
so that's definitely traditional.

00:38:19.895 --> 00:38:21.726
There's no metal, there's no screws,

00:38:21.726 --> 00:38:23.756
there's no nails, there's no braces

00:38:23.756 --> 00:38:25.756
that hold the canoe together.

00:38:25.756 --> 00:38:27.838
There's no navigation
equipment that we use

00:38:27.838 --> 00:38:29.755
to go across the ocean.

00:38:32.560 --> 00:38:34.796
When we are navigating the open ocean,

00:38:34.796 --> 00:38:37.324
our biggest clue that
tells us where we are

00:38:37.324 --> 00:38:39.234
and where we're going is the sun.

00:38:39.234 --> 00:38:42.434
The sun, as we know, rises in
the east and sets in the west,

00:38:42.434 --> 00:38:45.460
so if we just know where one point is,

00:38:45.460 --> 00:38:49.060
then we know where everything else is.

00:38:49.060 --> 00:38:50.748
The stars do us the same thing,

00:38:50.748 --> 00:38:52.364
because it's like the compass in the sky.

00:38:52.364 --> 00:38:55.716
We memorize close to 200
stars and know where they rise

00:38:55.716 --> 00:38:59.756
and where they set and how
they move across the skies.

00:38:59.756 --> 00:39:01.553
- [Bruce] The sun and
the stars and everything

00:39:01.553 --> 00:39:04.275
work in conjunction with the swells.

00:39:04.275 --> 00:39:06.305
So in the absence of the sun,

00:39:06.305 --> 00:39:08.553
then you maintain the orientation

00:39:08.553 --> 00:39:09.871
of the crew through swell patterns.

00:39:09.871 --> 00:39:12.371
That's a very difficult thing.

00:39:19.320 --> 00:39:23.129
- We started in Hawaii
in end of May, 2014.

00:39:23.129 --> 00:39:25.204
We embarked on this worldwide voyage,

00:39:25.204 --> 00:39:28.197
which first ended up going towards Tahiti

00:39:28.197 --> 00:39:29.582
through our ancestral routes,

00:39:29.582 --> 00:39:32.915
and now we're here in Martha's Vineyard.

00:39:35.761 --> 00:39:38.479
We see this overwhelming support

00:39:38.479 --> 00:39:41.199
from peoples all around the world.

00:39:41.199 --> 00:39:44.782
Hokule'a's the symbol
of hope and of pride.

00:39:53.156 --> 00:39:54.478
- A big part of this voyage, right,

00:39:54.478 --> 00:39:56.400
(speaking in foreign
language), is to train

00:39:56.400 --> 00:40:00.317
a whole cadre of young
navigators and captains.

00:40:01.982 --> 00:40:03.272
Wait, leave the back sail.

00:40:03.272 --> 00:40:05.657
Let's get the 23 out.

00:40:05.657 --> 00:40:06.907
A big foresail.

00:40:10.308 --> 00:40:14.017
Kaleo Wang is one of the
apprentice navigators.

00:40:14.017 --> 00:40:17.835
He understands that, you know, it's
going to take long hours

00:40:17.835 --> 00:40:19.472
and lack of sleep.

00:40:19.472 --> 00:40:23.225
He understands how to use all the tools

00:40:23.225 --> 00:40:24.975
that nature provides.

00:40:26.934 --> 00:40:28.780
Kaleo and these others, they're just like

00:40:28.780 --> 00:40:31.600
a pinch of salt as far as the people

00:40:31.600 --> 00:40:35.183
who know how to do this
type of navigation.

00:40:46.781 --> 00:40:48.412
- When I'm on Hokule'a,

00:40:48.412 --> 00:40:50.673
you know, and we're sailing in the open ocean,

00:40:50.673 --> 00:40:53.237
I feel definitely a deep sense of pride

00:40:53.237 --> 00:40:55.034
of what our ancestors were able to do

00:40:55.034 --> 00:40:56.747
and what we can still do today.

00:40:56.747 --> 00:40:58.346
When we're in the middle of the ocean,

00:40:58.346 --> 00:41:01.067
we're seeing the same swells
that they would have saw,

00:41:01.067 --> 00:41:02.927
the same stars that they would have

00:41:02.927 --> 00:41:04.175
been looking at and using,

00:41:04.175 --> 00:41:06.604
the same birds, the same
everything in the ocean.

00:41:06.604 --> 00:41:08.049
But when we're on the canoe,

00:41:08.049 --> 00:41:10.447
definitely connected more to
our culture, to our people,

00:41:10.447 --> 00:41:11.864
to our ancestors.

